},{"title":"Child Process Node.js v6.2.1-rc.1 Manual & Documentation","url":"https://nodejs.org/download/rc/v6.2.1-rc.1/docs/api/child_process.html"},{"title":"Child Process","url":"https://nodejs.org/download/release/v6.8.0/docs/api/child_process.html"},{"title":"Child Process","url":"https://nodejs.org/download/release/v8.6.0/docs/api/child_process.html"},{"title":"Child Process Node.js v4.0.0-rc.4 Manual & Documentation","url":"https://nodejs.org/download/rc/v4.0.0-rc.4/docs/api/child_process.html"},{"title":"Child Process","url":"https://nodejs.org/download/release/v8.2.1/docs/api/child_process.html"},{"title":"Child Process Node.js v0.11.13 Manual & Documentation","url":"https://nodejs.org/docs/v0.11.13/api/child_process.html"},{"title":"Child Process","url":"https://nodejs.org/download/rc/v6.11.4-rc.2/docs/api/child_process.html"}]},"I found the documentation for Node.js `child_process.spawnSync`. Here are the key details:\n\n## Overview\nThe spawnSync function provides equivalent functionality in a synchronous manner that blocks the event loop until the spawned process either exits or is terminated.\n\n## Basic Syntax\nThe function signature is `child_process.spawnSync(command[, args][, options])`\n\n## Synchronous Behavior\nThe child_process.spawnSync() method is synchronous and WILL block the Node.js event loop, pausing execution of any additional code until the spawned process exits.\n\n## Return Value\nThe method returns an object with properties including: pid (the process ID), output (array of results from stdio output), stdout (the contents of output[1]), and stderr (the contents of output[2])\n\n## Parameters and Options\nThe function accepts options for: cwd (current working directory), input (value passed as stdin), stdio configuration, env (environment variables), uid/gid (user/group identity), timeout (maximum time in milliseconds), killSignal (signal used when killing the process), and encoding\n\nThe official Node.js documentation can be found a